# Issue tracker: GitHub

Issues and PRDs for this repo live as GitHub Issues in `{{ github_org }}/{{ github_repo }}`. Use
the `gh` CLI for every operation.

## Conventions

- **Create an issue**: `gh issue create --title "..." --body "..."`. Use a heredoc for a
  multi-line body.
- **Read an issue**: `gh issue view <number> --comments`, filtering comments with `jq` and
  pulling the labels too.
- **List issues**: `gh issue list --state open --json number,title,body,labels,comments --jq '[.[] | {number, title, body, labels: [.labels[].name], comments: [.comments[].body]}]'`
  with the appropriate `--label` and `--state` filters.
- **Comment**: `gh issue comment <number> --body "..."`
- **Add / remove a label**: `gh issue edit <number> --add-label "..."` / `--remove-label "..."`
- **Close**: `gh issue close <number> --comment "..."`

The repo is inferred from `git remote -v` — `gh` handles that on its own inside the
clone.

Issue titles and bodies are in **pt-BR** (same language rule as
[`communication.md`](communication.md)). Label names and commands stay as they are.

This tracker is for the **product's own** code. A defect or improvement in
platform-owned code goes upstream to the template repository instead — the
`platform-feedback` skill (English body, `pnpm platform feedback`), never an issue here.

## Labels

Three axes, all closed lists — don't create a new label without the user's decision:

- **Type**: `bug` | `enhancement`.
- **Area** (one per issue, closed list): this product's own set, not the template's —
  list every label this repo actually carries under its area prefix. Confirm with
  `gh label list` before creating an issue rather than reusing an example from this file.
  Illustrative shape only (a generic product, not this one): `Billing` · `Onboarding` ·
  `Notifications` · `Reports` · `Access & login`. None fits → issue with no area label.
- **Size**: `Minor` | `Major`; normal size = **no** label. In the interactive flow, size
  is always a question to the user; automated triage always produces issues with no size
  label.
- **`triagem-ia`**: issue created by automated feedback triage — it means "investigated by
  a robot, awaiting human review". Never apply it by hand.

The full creation flow (interview, investigation, duplicate check, template) lives in the
`.agents/skills/creating-issues/SKILL.md` skill — that is the standard path for creating
an issue in this repo.

## Issues born from automated triage

Reports from the app's feedback form become issues through the
`.github/workflows/feedback-triage.yml` workflow. These issues carry a
footer with `Ambiente: <dev|production>`, `Relato: <id>` (the feedback id in the database
— the admin finds the report and its attachments on the feedback screen) and, when
available, a `correlationId` for tracing logs. A duplicate never becomes a new issue: the
robot comments on the equivalent open issue instead.

## Pull requests as a triage surface

**PRs as a request surface: no.** _(Switch to `yes` if this repo starts treating external
PRs as feature requests; `/triage` reads this flag.)_

This is also why our own work never opens a PR — changes merge locally into `main`. See
[`workflow.md`](workflow.md).

With `yes`, PRs go through the same labels and states as issues, using the `gh pr`
equivalents:

- **Read a PR**: `gh pr view <number> --comments`, and `gh pr diff <number>` for the diff.
- **List external PRs for triage**: `gh pr list --state open --json number,title,body,labels,author,authorAssociation,comments`,
  keeping only `authorAssociation` in `CONTRIBUTOR`, `FIRST_TIME_CONTRIBUTOR` or `NONE`
  (discard `OWNER`/`MEMBER`/`COLLABORATOR`).
- **Comment / label / close**: `gh pr comment`, `gh pr edit --add-label`/`--remove-label`,
  `gh pr close`.

GitHub shares a single numbering space between issues and PRs, so `#42` may be either —
resolve with `gh pr view 42` and fall back to `gh issue view 42`.

## When a skill says "publish to the issue tracker"

Create a GitHub issue.

## When a skill says "fetch the relevant ticket"

Run `gh issue view <number> --comments`.

## Wayfinding operations

Used by `/wayfinder`. The **map** is a single issue with **child** issues as tickets.

- **Map**: an issue labelled `wayfinder:map`, holding the Notes / Decisions-so-far / Fog
  body. `gh issue create --label wayfinder:map`.
- **Child ticket**: an issue linked to the map as a GitHub sub-issue (`gh api` against the
  sub-issues endpoint). Where sub-issues aren't enabled, add the child to a task list in
  the map's body and put `Part of #<map>` at the top of the child's body. Labels:
  `wayfinder:<type>` (`research`/`prototype`/`grilling`/`task`). Once claimed, the ticket
  is assigned to the dev working it.
- **Blocking**: native GitHub **issue dependencies** — the canonical representation, and
  visible in the UI. Add the edge with
  `gh api --method POST repos/{{ github_org }}/{{ github_repo }}/issues/<child>/dependencies/blocked_by -F issue_id=<blocker-db-id>`,
  where `<blocker-db-id>` is the blocker's numeric **database id**
  (`gh api repos/{{ github_org }}/{{ github_repo }}/issues/<n> --jq .id` — _not_ the `#number` and
  not the `node_id`). GitHub reports `issue_dependencies_summary.blocked_by` (open
  blockers only — the live gate). Where dependencies don't exist, fall back to a
  `Blocked by: #<n>, #<n>` line at the top of the child's body. A ticket is unblocked when
  every blocker is closed.
- **Frontier query**: list the map's open children (`gh issue list --state open`, scoped to
  the map's sub-issues / task list), discard those with an open blocker
  (`issue_dependencies_summary.blocked_by > 0`, or an open issue on the `Blocked by` line)
  or with an assignee; the first one in the map's order wins.
- **Claim**: `gh issue edit <n> --add-assignee @me` — the session's first write.
- **Resolve**: `gh issue comment <n> --body "<answer>"`, then `gh issue close <n>`, then
  attach a context pointer (gist + link) to the map's Decisions-so-far.
